<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunthorn Phu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sunthorn Phu was a Thai poet of the early Rattanakosin period, often called the Bard of Rattanakosin. A favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, he fell from favour under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>, became a monk and travelled widely, writing travel poems known as nirat. His masterpiece is the long verse romance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-aphai-mani">Phra Aphai Mani</a>. In 1986 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> honoured him on the bicentenary of his birth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu.jpg" alt="Sunthorn Phu" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: กสิณธร ราชโอรส · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_(28).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
